From: fil-c-utils maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Align the libcrux Result payload for Fil-C

OpenSSH 10.5p1's generated libcrux code returns an eight-byte array from a
tagged Result whose payload begins at offset one. Fil-C 0.684's aarch64 calling
convention lowering incorrectly represents every aggregate of at most sixteen
bytes as capability pointers, even when its natural alignment is less than
eight bytes. Returning this valid byte-aligned Result therefore emits an
eight-byte-aligned pointer load and panics at runtime. Fil-C fixes the compiler
bug in e0ded6df4296a6b1170f19e5faedaa7c08b90a02.

Align only this payload for AArch64 Fil-C releases that predate the compiler
fix, and assert the layout on those builds so generated-source drift fails
closed. Other targets and compilers retain the generated layout.

--- a/libcrux_internal.h
+++ b/libcrux_internal.h
@@ -3600,10 +3600,18 @@ typedef struct core_result_Result_8e_s
     Eurydice_array_u8x8 case_Ok;
     core_array_TryFromSliceError case_Err;
   }
+#if defined(__FILC__) && defined(__aarch64__)
+  __attribute__((aligned(8)))
+#endif
   val;
 }
 core_result_Result_8e;
 
+#if defined(__FILC__) && defined(__aarch64__)
+_Static_assert(__builtin_offsetof(core_result_Result_8e, val) == 8,
+    "libcrux Result payload must be 8-byte aligned");
+#endif
+
 /**
 This function found in impl {core::result::Result<T, E>[TraitClause@0, TraitClause@1]}
 */
